Ontology (@ontology: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #part-of @first-steps, #done) { r_S8PQ17MQ3WBWM53ZN700K89V9F

Ontology is the study of being and existence. When we ask ourselves what data I have in my Lince, what kind of data, how do i manage such data, how do I share data in general, integrate with other Lince/systems, we are talking about Ontology.

We have a Record, it can be given a slug (nickname) as @my-record. Records can have assertions that use a Lingua Vocabulary, it is a way for people to make sure they are talking about the same thing, when you attatch #done to a Record, if in my Vocabulary/Ontology it means the same thing, we can share workflows that have predictable consequences, we can collaborate better. When I make an assertion alone, i am applying a Concept to a Record, 'Eating Lunch' #done, when i assert with another Record, I am making a link: 'Eat Apple' #part-of @nutrition (the slug of the 'Nutrition' Record).

Your Lince is a Cell, you can make a public facing entity that is called an Organ, and a bunch of devices/Cells you have can be in that Organ. The Organs you participate in can be of your public profile, work, family, friends, hobbies...

One can sync their data to a file, or to other Organs, controlling with Protein (used in the interface to get data too), filtering what is sent of Records/Karma/Concepts and what properties of them, to what Organ.

Ontology has these parts and many more. They manage how people interact, the meanings and connections. The other parts of Lince are focused on specific workflows, of Transfers between people, automations with Karma and interactions of Interface.

} r_S8PQ17MQ3WBWM53ZN700K89V9F


